              {
                "text": "I'm testing whether horizontal or vertical runs of 8s of length 3 map to specific patterns, but the transformations aren't lining up consistently between the two grids yet.\n\nNow I'm trying a different approach: counting the number of 8-neighbors each 8-cell has, checking if degree 1 corresponds to a 2 and degree 2 corresponds to a 5, working through each cell in grid2 to test this hypothesis.\n\nBut I'm finding contradictions - some degree-1 cells map to 5 and some degree-2 cells map to 2, so I need to look at connected components instead. Grouping the 8s into connected clusters and checking if each cluster's output pattern relates to position within the shape, but the mapping still looks inconsistent, so I'm now considering whether symmetry, like comparing the grid to its transpose, might explain the pattern instead.\n\nTesting 180-degree rotation symmetry didn't quite fit either, but checking vertical mirror symmetry (row i against row 5-i) seems promising: cells that are 8 and whose vertical mirror is also 8 map to 2, while cells whose mirror is 0 map to 5, and this pattern is holding up across several test cases so far.\n\nThe remaining pairs all check out at 0, confirming the vertical mirror symmetry holds throughout. Now I want to verify this pattern holds more generally, not just for this specific grid.",
